# Core / Project Boundary and Structure Rules

## Purpose

These rules define the boundary between framework core and project-specific implementation.

They also define naming, file structure, and import conventions.

The goal is to keep the framework reusable, predictable, and easy for AI agents to extend without guessing architecture.

---

## Main Principle

Framework core provides reusable mechanisms.

Project layer provides concrete product implementation.

Do not make framework core aware of one specific application, domain, product, user role, endpoint, selector, or business workflow.

---

## Rule Priority

When creating or modifying files, follow this priority:

1. Project map.
2. Core / Project Boundary rules.
3. More specific rules for the affected layer.
4. General framework rules.
5. Examples from rules only as illustrative patterns.

If a generic example conflicts with the project map or existing architecture, follow the project map.

Do not create files, folders, classes, tags, commands, roles, endpoints, or abstractions only because they appear in examples.

---

# Core vs Project Boundary

## Framework Core Owns Mechanisms

Framework core may contain reusable mechanisms that can work across projects.

Allowed in framework core:

- base test setup;
- base fixtures;
- generic fixture composition mechanisms;
- typed configuration loading mechanisms;
- environment configuration infrastructure;
- logger;
- reporting integrations;
- trace/artifact helpers;
- generic API transport;
- generic request context wrappers;
- generic auth provider interfaces;
- generic token/session cache mechanisms;
- generic credentials provider interfaces;
- generic assertion helpers;
- generic data generators;
- generic utilities;
- Playwright setup mechanisms;
- quality gate integration helpers;
- common TypeScript types that are not domain-specific.

---

## Project Layer Owns Product Behavior

Project layer owns concrete application behavior.

Project layer should contain:

- specs;
- Page Objects;
- Component Objects;
- concrete fixtures;
- project-specific data fixtures;
- project-specific builders;
- project-specific generators;
- datasets;
- domain models;
- business flows;
- concrete API clients;
- concrete endpoints;
- concrete users;
- concrete roles;
- credentials references;
- selectors;
- domain assertions;
- project-specific tags;
- storage states for concrete project users;
- setup flows for concrete project scenarios.

---

## Not Allowed in Framework Core

Framework core must not contain:

- project-specific Page Objects;
- project-specific Component Objects;
- concrete business flows;
- concrete user roles;
- concrete credentials;
- concrete users;
- concrete endpoints;
- domain models;
- domain assertions;
- selectors for a specific product;
- project-specific test data;
- project-specific datasets;
- project-specific builders;
- project-specific generators;
- feature-specific fixtures;
- product-specific tags;
- product-specific workflows;
- hardcoded URLs;
- hardcoded tokens;
- hardcoded passwords;
- hardcoded environment values.

---

## Allowed Core Examples

Good examples of core-level abstractions:

```text
BaseFixture
ConfigProvider
Logger
ApiTransport
AuthProvider
TokenCache
CredentialsProvider
ReporterAdapter
ArtifactHelper
RetryPolicy
DateGenerator
StringGenerator
SchemaValidator
```

These are mechanisms.

They do not know the concrete product domain.

---

## Not Allowed Core Examples

Bad examples in framework core:

```text
LoginPage
CheckoutPage
ProductsPage
AdminUser
BuyerUser
SellerUser
OrderBuilder
ProductBuilder
CheckoutFlow
OrderApiClient
/catalog endpoint
/checkout endpoint
@checkout tag
```

These are product-specific concepts.

They belong to the project layer.

---

## Auth Boundary

Framework core may provide generic authentication mechanisms.

Allowed in core:

```text
AuthProvider
AuthStrategy
TokenCache
CredentialsProvider
AuthHeaderFactory
StorageStateManager
```

Project layer owns concrete authentication details:

```text
admin user
buyer user
seller role
login endpoint
tenant-specific credentials
project storage state
project role matrix
```

Core may know how authentication works technically.

Core must not know who the concrete project users are.

---

## API Boundary

Framework core may provide:

- generic API transport;
- request context creation;
- base URL injection mechanism;
- auth header mechanism;
- response parsing utilities;
- generic schema validation utilities.

Project layer owns:

- concrete API clients;
- endpoint paths;
- request payload builders;
- response domain types;
- endpoint-specific assertions;
- workflow-specific API setup;
- domain-specific contracts.

Bad in core:

```text
OrderClient
ProductClient
UserManagementClient
createOrder()
approveOrder()
cancelCheckout()
```

Good in core:

```text
ApiClient
ApiTransport
RequestOptions
AuthHeaders
ResponseParser
```

---

## UI Boundary

Page Objects and Component Objects are normally project-specific.

Framework core must not contain Page Objects or Component Objects for one concrete product.

Project layer owns:

- pages;
- components;
- selectors;
- navigation paths;
- UI-specific actions;
- structural page assertions;
- project-specific UI flows.

Allowed in core only if truly generic:

- base UI helper types;
- locator helper types;
- generic accessibility helpers;
- generic screenshot helpers;
- generic visual comparison mechanism;
- generic Playwright utilities.

---

## Fixture Boundary

Core fixtures must be infrastructure-focused.

Allowed in core fixtures:

```text
config
logger
apiTransport
authProvider
testInfoHelper
artifactHelper
```

Project fixtures may expose:

```text
loginPage
homePage
productsPage
userBuilder
orderBuilder
projectApiClient
projectAuthState
```

Do not expose project-specific pages, users, roles, endpoints, or business flows from framework core fixtures.

---

## Data Boundary

Core may contain generic data mechanisms.

Allowed in core:

- generic random string generator;
- generic email generator;
- generic date helper;
- generic ID generator;
- generic builder base type if it is truly domain-neutral.

Project layer owns domain data:

- user builder;
- product builder;
- order builder;
- address dataset;
- payment data;
- role matrix;
- project-specific schemas;
- domain-specific payloads.

---

# Naming and File Structure Rules

## Purpose

Naming should make ownership obvious.

File structure should make the layer obvious.

AI agents must follow existing project structure and the project map before creating new files.

---

## General Naming Rules

- Use names that describe ownership and responsibility.
- Avoid vague names such as `helper`, `utils`, `common`, `manager`, `service`, or `misc` unless the responsibility is clearly defined.
- Do not create duplicate abstractions with different names.
- Do not create new folders just to group one file.
- Do not create new naming patterns when an existing convention exists.
- Prefer explicit names over generic names.
- Prefer project-approved path aliases over deep relative imports.

---

## Recommended File Suffixes

Use consistent suffixes when the project map does not define another convention.

Recommended:

```text
*.page.ts          -> Page Objects
*.component.ts     -> Component Objects
*.fixture.ts       -> Playwright fixtures
*.builder.ts       -> test data builders
*.generator.ts     -> data generators
*.dataset.ts       -> static or reusable datasets
*.client.ts        -> API clients
*.provider.ts      -> providers such as config/auth/credentials
*.flow.ts          -> project-level domain or workflow flows
*.schema.ts        -> schemas or contract definitions
*.type.ts          -> shared types
*.spec.ts          -> tests
*.rules.md         -> rule files
*.mdc              -> Cursor rule files when applicable
```

Do not introduce a suffix that duplicates an existing convention.

---

## Recommended Class Names

Examples of clear class names:

```text
LoginPage
AccountPage
HeaderComponent
FilterPanelComponent
ResultListComponent
UserBuilder
EmailGenerator
ApiClient
AuthProvider
CheckoutFlow
ConfigProvider
```

Avoid names like:

```text
CommonHelper
TestUtils
PageManager
FlowManager
GeneralService
MainHelper
BaseEverything
```

unless the responsibility is precise and justified.

---

## Page Object Naming

Page Objects should be named after a route, screen, page, or navigation boundary.

Good:

```text
LoginPage
AccountPage
ProductsPage
CheckoutPage
OrderDetailsPage
```

Avoid:

```text
SortingPage
SubmitButtonPage
HeaderPage
ProductCardPage
```

These are usually components or page parts, not pages.

---

## Component Object Naming

Component Objects should be named after meaningful UI blocks.

Good:

```text
HeaderComponent
SidebarComponent
ToastComponent
ModalComponent
FilterPanelComponent
SortComponent
ResultListComponent
TableComponent
CartSummaryComponent
```

Avoid creating components for:

```text
SubmitButtonComponent
EmailInputComponent
SingleLinkComponent
OneTextComponent
```

unless the element has meaningful reusable behavior.

---

## API Naming

API clients should represent endpoint groups or resource areas, not workflows.

Good:

```text
UserClient
OrderClient
CatalogClient
AuthClient
```

Avoid:

```text
CreateAndApproveOrderService
FullCheckoutApiService
LoginAndPrepareUserClient
```

If multiple API calls represent a business workflow, use a project-level flow only when justified.

Do not create complex service hierarchies by default.

---

## Builder and Generator Naming

Builders create valid domain data by default.

Generators create primitive or unique values.

Good:

```text
UserBuilder
OrderBuilder
EmailGenerator
IdGenerator
DateGenerator
```

Avoid:

```text
RandomHelper
DataUtils
FakeManager
CommonBuilder
```

Project-specific builders belong to the project layer.

Generic primitive generators may live in core only when domain-neutral.

---

## Flow Naming

Flows are project-specific and should describe reusable business workflows.

Good:

```text
AuthFlow
CheckoutFlow
OrderCreationFlow
ProductPurchaseFlow
```

Avoid:

```text
ClickFlow
SortingFlow
ButtonFlow
ModalFlow
```

Simple page or component behavior does not need a flow.

Do not introduce a domain flow layer unless repeated cross-page business workflows justify it.

---

# File Placement Rules

## Follow Project Map First

Before creating a file, check:

- project map;
- existing folder conventions;
- existing naming conventions;
- path aliases;
- nearby similar files;
- relevant rule files.

Do not invent structure if the project already has a convention.

---

## Suggested Structure

If the project map does not define another structure, use this model:

```text
framework-core/
  fixtures/
  config/
  api/
  auth/
  logging/
  reporting/
  utils/
  assertions/
  types/

project/
  fixtures/
  pages/
  components/
  test-data/
    builders/
    generators/
    datasets/
    types/
  api/
    clients/
    schemas/
    types/
  domain/
  specs/
```

This structure is a guideline.

The actual project map is the source of truth.

---

## Framework Core File Placement

Place in framework core only if the file is reusable across projects.

Examples:

```text
framework-core/config/config-provider.ts
framework-core/api/api-transport.ts
framework-core/auth/auth-provider.ts
framework-core/logging/logger.ts
framework-core/fixtures/base.fixture.ts
framework-core/utils/date-generator.ts
```

Do not place project-specific files in framework core.

---

## Project File Placement

Place product-specific files in the project layer.

Examples:

```text
project/pages/login.page.ts
project/pages/account.page.ts
project/components/header.component.ts
project/test-data/builders/user.builder.ts
project/test-data/generators/email.generator.ts
project/api/clients/order.client.ts
project/domain/checkout.flow.ts
project/specs/ui/account/account.spec.ts
```

---

# Import Rules

## Import From Public Entry Points

Specs should import from approved public entry points.

Good:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@project/fixtures/test";
```

Avoid:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
```

Avoid importing from internal fixture layers unless the project map explicitly allows it.

---

## Prefer Path Aliases

Use path aliases when the project map defines them.

Good:

```ts
import { LoginPage } from "@project/pages/login.page";
```

Avoid deep relative paths when aliases exist:

```ts
import { LoginPage } from "../../../../src/project/pages/login.page";
```

If aliases are not configured, follow the existing repository convention.

Do not invent aliases.

---

## Do Not Bypass Layers

Do not import project-specific code into framework core.

Bad:

```ts
// framework-core/auth/auth-provider.ts
import { adminUser } from "@project/test-data/users.dataset";
```

Good:

```ts
// project/fixtures/auth.fixture.ts
import { adminUser } from "@project/test-data/users.dataset";
```

Core can define interfaces.

Project layer can provide concrete implementations.

---

# Creation Rules

## Before Creating Any New Abstraction

Check whether an existing abstraction can be reused or extended.

Check:

- Page Objects;
- Component Objects;
- fixtures;
- builders;
- generators;
- API clients;
- providers;
- utilities;
- flows;
- project map.

Do not create duplicate abstractions with overlapping responsibilities.

---

## Create in Core Only When

A new file belongs in core only when:

- it is reusable across projects;
- it does not know the product domain;
- it does not contain selectors;
- it does not contain concrete endpoints;
- it does not contain concrete roles or users;
- it does not contain business flows;
- it provides a mechanism rather than a scenario.

---

## Create in Project Layer When

A new file belongs in project layer when it contains:

- selectors;
- routes;
- Page Objects;
- Component Objects;
- business workflows;
- concrete API endpoints;
- project-specific data;
- domain models;
- concrete roles;
- concrete users;
- domain assertions;
- feature specs.

---

# Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- project-specific logic in framework core;
- Page Objects in framework core for one concrete app;
- Component Objects in framework core for one concrete app;
- hardcoded URLs, tokens, credentials, or environment values;
- duplicate builders with different names;
- duplicate API clients for the same endpoint group;
- generic `utils` folders full of unrelated logic;
- generic wrappers around Playwright without clear value;
- deep relative imports when aliases exist;
- creating folders before a real grouping need appears;
- naming files after implementation details instead of responsibility;
- moving business flows into Page Objects;
- moving project-specific auth details into core;
- creating abstractions only because examples show them.

---

# Review Checklist

Before accepting architecture, naming, or structure changes, verify:

- the project map was followed;
- the file belongs to the correct layer;
- core does not contain project-specific behavior;
- project-specific code does not leak into core;
- names clearly describe responsibility;
- no duplicate abstraction was introduced;
- no speculative abstraction was added;
- imports follow approved entry points and aliases;
- no hardcoded secrets or environment values were added;
- no product-specific roles, users, endpoints, selectors, or flows were added to core.

---

# Final Principle

If the code is reusable infrastructure, it may belong in framework core.

If the code knows the product, page, user, role, endpoint, selector, data model, or business workflow, it belongs in the project layer.

When unsure, keep the implementation in the project layer until a real reusable core mechanism is proven.